      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellflower%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Bellflower apple, chosen by Jesse Richards who loved the fruit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellflower%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1874, a railroad ran through Bellflower, the same tracks that run today, linking it to regional grain markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://villageofbellflower.org/history",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bellflower quickly became a major grain shipping center, moving over 350,000 bushels a year by 1879.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellflower%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
